General elections were held in [[Panama]] on 8 May 1994, electing both a new President of the Republic and a new Legislative Assembly.

[[Ernesto Pérez Balladares]] stood as a candidate for the [[Democratic Revolutionary Party]] (PRD), opposing [[Mireya Moscoso]] of the [[Arnulfista Party]] and the salsa singer [[Rubén Blades]], who was then president of the party [[Papa Egoro]]. In the [[1989 Panamanian general election|1989 general election]], Pérez Balladares had served as the campaign manager for [[Carlos Duque]], the hand-picked candidate of military ruler [[Manuel Noriega]], and his 1994 opponents sought to emphasize his connection with Noriega, broadcasting pictures of the two together.<ref name=NYT1>{{cite web |url=https://www.nytimes.com/1994/02/21/world/panama-journal-democracy-at-work-under-shadow-of-dictators.html?pagewanted=all&src=pm |title=Panama Journal; Democracy at Work, Under Shadow of Dictators |author=Howard W. French |date=February 21, 1994 |work=The New York Times |archivedate=August 8, 2014 |url-status=live |archiveurl=https://web.archive.org/web/20140808184548/http://www.nytimes.com/1994/02/21/world/panama-journal-democracy-at-work-under-shadow-of-dictators.html?pagewanted=all&src=pm |accessdate=September 2, 2012}}</ref> Pérez Balladares denied the link, describing the current PRD as "diametrically opposed" to Noriega's policies.<ref name=WP1 /> Instead, he worked to position himself as a successor to Torrijos, who was regarded as a national hero. The incumbent Arnulfista Party, meanwhile, was seen as hobbled by dissatisfaction with the perceived incompetence and corruption of Endara's government.<ref name=WP1>{{cite news |url=http://www.highbeam.com/doc/1P2-889827.html |title=Panamanians Vote in Peace, Picking Ex-Aide of Noriega; Millionaire Perez Balladares Bests Widow of Four-Time President |author=Douglas Farah |date=May 9, 1994 |newspaper=The Washington Post |archive-date=March 29, 2015 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20150329104016/http://www.highbeam.com/doc/1P2-889827.html |via=[[HighBeam Research]] |url-access=subscription |url-status=usurped |access-date=September 2, 2012}}</ref> He ultimately won the election with 33% of the vote, with Moscoso receiving 29% and Blades receiving 17%.<ref>{{cite web |url=http://www.umsl.edu/services/govdocs/wofact98/193.htm |title=Panama |publisher=University of Missouri-Saint Louis |archivedate=October 23, 2012 |url-status=dead |archiveurl=https://web.archive.org/web/20121023195301/http://www.umsl.edu/services/govdocs/wofact98/193.htm |accessdate=September 2, 2012}}</ref>

The PRD "also achieved an effective majority in the new National Assembly. The big surprise was not the victory of the PRD, but the nearly successful challenge of Mireya Moscoso de Gruber, the candidate of the Arnulfista Party".<ref>Falcoff, Mark. The 1994 Panamanian elections: post-election report. Washington: CSIS Americas Program. 1994. Pp. 1.
</ref>

